\subsubsection{Event Selection} 
  The event selection is similar to Run I as described in~Section~\ref{sec:events_selection} with following exceptions mentioned below
  \begin{itemize}
   \item{\bf FSR Photons} are attached with leptons at Run II while with $\Z_{1}/\Z_{2}$ at Run I.
   \item {\bf 'smart cut'}: defining $\Z_{a}$ and $ Z_{b}$ as
     the mass-sorted alternative pairing Z candidates
     (${\rm Z_a}$ being the one closest to the nominal Z boson mass),
     require NOT($|m_{\rm Za}-m_{\rm Z}| < |m_{\rm Z1}-m_{\rm Z}|$ AND $m_{\rm Zb}<12$).
     Selected FSR photons are included in $m_{\rm Z}$'s computations.
     This cut discards $4\mu$ and $4e$ candidates where the alternative pairing
     looks like an on-shell Z + low-mass $\ell^+ \ell^-$.
     (NB. In Run I, such a situation was avoided by choosing the best ZZ candidate
     before applying kinematic cuts to it, most precisely before the $m_{\rm Z2} > 12~\GeVcc$ cut.
     The present smart cut allows to choose the best ZZ candidate after all kinematic cuts.)
     \item Unlike in the Run I analysis, the best ZZ candidate is now chosen after all kinematic cuts, a change that allows to test other
     selection strategies for this candidate choice. This is especially relevant for events with more than four selected leptons, having
     in mind the search for the Higgs boson production modes where associated particles can decay to leptons, such as VH and ttH.
  \end{itemize}
 Figure~\ref{fig:Mass4l} for the full dataset, and compared to
expectations from the SM backgrounds, first for the full mass range,
and then zooming on the low-mass range.

 \begin{figure}[!htb]
\vspace*{0.3cm}
